<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="A2.T6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">More results of DiT-XL/2 on class-conditional image generation.<span id="A2.T6.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The arrow denotes whether lower or higher values indicate superior performance. <math id="A2.T6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the number of cache intervals and is used to adjust the acceleration budget. All speeds are measured on RTX 4090 GPU.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>More Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply the SODA model to DiT‑XL/2, PixArt-<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and OpenSora, which represent category‑to‑image, text‑to‑image, and text‑to‑video generation tasks, respectively.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.1.1 </span>DiT-XL/2</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments conducted on DiT-XL/2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> employ both DDPM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and DDIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> samplers. The DDPM and DDIM samplers that we adopt consist of 250 and 50 steps, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For DDPM, implementation of different methods is as follows: FORA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> adopts a fixed caching interval of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
ToCa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> employs an average caching ratio of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=93\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>93</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=93\%</annotation></semantics></math> and an average caching interval of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=4</annotation></semantics></math>, and DuCa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite> employs a caching ratio of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=95\%</annotation></semantics></math> and an average caching interval of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
For our method SODA, we perform a pre-inference error analysis using 100 images, setting <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.4</annotation></semantics></math>. The overall acceleration efficiency is controlled via the number of caching intervals, denoted by <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For DDIM, FORA adopts a fixed caching interval of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=3</annotation></semantics></math>. ToCa employs a caching ratio of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=93\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>93</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=93\%</annotation></semantics></math> and an average caching interval of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=3</annotation></semantics></math>, and DuCa employs an average caching ratio of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=95\%</annotation></semantics></math> and an average caching interval of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=3</annotation></semantics></math>. The basic settings of our SODA are kept consistent with those in DDPM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.1.2 </span>PixArt</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For PixArt-<math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, we adopt the DPM++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> with 20 steps as sampler. The implementation of different methods is as follows: FORA adopts a fixed caching interval of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. ToCa employs an average caching ratio of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=70\%</annotation></semantics></math> and an average caching interval of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=3</annotation></semantics></math>, and DuCa employs an average caching ratio of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=25\%</annotation></semantics></math> and an average caching interval of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=3</annotation></semantics></math>. For our method SODA, we perform a pre-inference error analysis using 100 images, setting <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>. The overall acceleration efficiency is controlled via the number of caching intervals, denoted by <math id="A2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.1.3 </span>OpenSora</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments on OpenSora are conducted with 30 reflective flow steps. The implementation of different methods is as follows:
FORA adopts a fixed caching interval of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. ToCa sets different average caching intervals for different modules, with <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=3</annotation></semantics></math> for temporal attention, spatial attention, and MLP, and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=6</annotation></semantics></math> for cross-attention. The average caching ratio is set to <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> for all modules except the MLP, which has an average caching ratio of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>85</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85\%</annotation></semantics></math>. DuCa adopts the same scheme as ToCa. <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>-DiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> divides the complete denoising process into two stages. PAB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> sets the caching interval to <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=2</annotation></semantics></math> for temporal attention, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=4</annotation></semantics></math> for spatial attention, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=6</annotation></semantics></math> for cross-attention. The MLP module is fully computed.
For our method SODA, we perform a pre-inference error analysis using 10 videos to reduce the time cost of error analysis, setting <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.0</annotation></semantics></math> for higher acceleration. The overall acceleration efficiency is controlled via the number of caching intervals, denoted by <math id="A2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.1.4 </span>Evaluation and metrics</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For class-conditional image generation, we use DiT-XL/2 to randomly generate 50k <math id="A2.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> images from ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, use FID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, sFID, IS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, Precision, and Recall to measure the quality of the generated images.
The Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) measures the distributional discrepancy between real and generated images in the Inception‑V3 feature space, where a lower score indicates higher fidelity and realism. Its spatial variant, sFID, computes feature distribution differences at the spatial level rather than the global pooling layer, making it more sensitive to structural and texture coherence. The Inception Score (IS) evaluates image quality and diversity based on the confidence and entropy of predicted class probabilities from an Inception network. A higher score implies clearer and more diverse generations. Precision quantifies perceptual fidelity by measuring how many generated samples fall within the manifold of real data, while Recall evaluates diversity by estimating the coverage of the real image distribution. Together, these metrics provide a comprehensive evaluation of the trade‑off between fidelity and diversity in class‑conditional image generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For text‑to‑image generation, we evaluate performance using FID‑30K and the CLIP Score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, which jointly assess generation quality and text‑image alignment of images generated by PixArt-<math id="A2.SS1.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. FID‑30K is computed between 30,000 generated images and the corresponding real images from the MS‑COCO2017 dataset<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, measuring the Fréchet distance between their feature distributions in the Inception‑V3 embedding space. A lower value indicates that the generated images are closer to real ones, reflecting higher visual fidelity and realism. The CLIP Score evaluates semantic consistency between an image and its associated text prompt by computing the cosine similarity of their representations in the CLIP model’s joint vision‑language embedding space. A higher CLIP Score denotes stronger semantic alignment and better text‑image correspondence. Together, these two metrics provide complementary perspectives on both the perceptual quality and the semantic faithfulness of text‑to‑image generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For text‑to‑video generation, we adopt VBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> to comprehensively assess the quality of generated videos. We use 950 text prompts and generate five videos per prompt, resulting in a total of 4,750 videos, each rendered at 480p resolution with a 9:16 aspect ratio and 2 seconds duration. VBench evaluates 16 aspects covering semantic alignment, visual quality, temporal consistency, and aesthetic appeal, providing a holistic measurement of video generation performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>More Experiment Results</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To supplement the experimental results presented in the main paper and further demonstrate the effectiveness of our method, this section includes additional acceleration results and ablation studies.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.2.1 </span>More results of Dit-XL/2</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide additional results of the SODA method under various acceleration ratios in Tab. <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix B More Experiments ‣ SODA: Sensitivity-Oriented Dynamic Acceleration for Diffusion Transformer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It can be observed that our method preserves most of the model performance under low acceleration ratios. Due to the minimal accumulated cache error obtained via dynamic programming and the adaptive pruning decision that further reduces the cache error, our method can even improve the performance of the original model in certain cases. For example, the results of DDPM with <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>=125 and DDIM with <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>=31.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, as the acceleration ratio continues to increase, the model inevitably begins to omit important computations after removing redundant ones, leading to a gradual degradation in performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This phenomenon reflects the natural trade-off between generation quality and acceleration. The advantage of our method lies in its ability to improve model performance under low acceleration ratios and significantly delay the trade-off point, thereby preserving more of the model’s performance even as the acceleration increases.</p>
</div>
